			<h3 class="ecl-u-type-heading-3" id="types-energy-consumed">Types of energy consumed</h3>

<p class="ecl-u-type-paragraph">In 2024, out of the&nbsp;<button class="estat-tooltip" data-toggle="tooltip" data-trigger="click focus" role="button" title="Total energy available = gross inland energy consumption, which means the overall supply of energy for all activities on the territory of the country or region, but excluding international maritime bunkers. For more details, see the glossary entry 'Gross inland energy consumption' in the end of this publication.">total energy available</button> in the EU, 66% was&nbsp;<button class="estat-tooltip" data-toggle="tooltip" data-trigger="click focus" role="button" title="Final energy consumption is the total energy consumed by end users, such as households, industry and agriculture.">consumed by end users</button> (final energy consumption), for example EU households, industry, and transport. The remaining 34% was mainly lost during electricity generation and distribution, used to support energy production processes, or consumed in non-energy uses (like asphalt or bitumen).</p>

<h4 class="ecl-u-type-heading-6">Petroleum products are the most consumed</h4>

<p class="ecl-u-type-paragraph">In the EU in 2024,&nbsp;<button class="estat-tooltip" data-toggle="tooltip" data-trigger="click focus" role="button" title="Total petroleum products are fossil fuels (usually in liquid state) and include crude oil and all products derived from it (e.g. when processed in oil refineries), including motor gasoline, diesel oil, fuel oil, etc.">petroleum products</button> (such as heating oil, petrol, diesel fuel) represented 37% of final energy consumption, followed by electricity (23%), natural and manufactured gas (20%), direct use of&nbsp;<button class="estat-tooltip" data-toggle="tooltip" data-trigger="click focus" role="button" title="Renewable energy sources, also called renewables, are energy sources that replenish (or renew) themselves naturally.">renewables</button> (not transformed into electricity, for example wood, solar thermal, geothermal or biogas for space heating or hot water production; 12%),&nbsp;<button class="estat-tooltip" data-toggle="tooltip" data-trigger="click focus" role="button" title="Derived heat is used for warming spaces and for industrial processes and is obtained by burning combustible fuels like coal, natural gas, oil, renewables (biofuels) and wastes, or also by transforming electricity to heat in electric boilers or heat pumps.">derived heat</button> (such as district heating; 5%) and&nbsp;<button class="estat-tooltip" data-toggle="tooltip" data-trigger="click focus" role="button" title="Solid fuels include here 1) fossil fuels covering various types of coals and solid products derived from coals, 2) peat and peat products, 3) oil shale and oil sands and 4) non-renewable waste.">solid fuels</button> (mostly coal; 2%).</p>

<p class="ecl-u-type-paragraph">The real consumption of renewable energy (25%) was higher than the direct use (12%) because other renewable sources, such as hydropower, wind power and solar photovoltaic, are included in electricity use.</p>

<p class="ecl-u-type-paragraph">Within the EU countries, the final energy consumption pattern varied considerably in 2024. Petroleum products made up more than 50% of final energy consumption in Cyprus, Greece, Luxembourg, Malta and Ireland. Electricity accounted the highest share of 41%&nbsp;in Malta. For&nbsp;gas, the largest share was 33% in the Netherlands. The direct use of renewable energies represented 28% of final energy consumption in both Latvia and Finland. The derived heat recorded the largest share in Denmark 21%.</p>
